SHOPSPHERE — STEPS EXPLAINED 
==============================================

This document explains every step in the `steps` file in plain language,
so you understand *why* each command exists and can recreate a similar
project yourself.

---

PREREQUISITES — What you need installed before you start
---------------------------------------------------------

  - Docker         → builds your code into a portable package (image)
  - kubectl        → the command-line tool to talk to Kubernetes
  - eksctl         → a tool that creates/manages EKS clusters easily
  - AWS CLI        → lets your terminal talk to AWS services
  - AWS Load Balancer Controller prereqs → IAM policy + OIDC provider
    (these are one-time AWS account setups; the steps show how)

WHY: Without these tools installed, your computer cannot build images,
connect to AWS, or control a Kubernetes cluster.

---

STEP 1 — BUILD AND PUSH DOCKER IMAGES TO DOCKERHUB
----------------------------------------------------

  # Build each service
  docker build -t bigkola1/easy-gateway:latest ./api-gateway
  docker build -t bigkola1/easy-frontend:latest ./frontend
  docker build -t bigkola1/easy-user-service:latest ./user-service
  docker build -t bigkola1/easy-product-service:latest ./product-service
  docker build -t bigkola1/easy-order-service:latest ./order-service

EXPLANATION:
  - `docker build` reads a Dockerfile in each service folder and creates
    a Docker image (a ready-to-run package of code + dependencies).
  - `-t bigkola1/gateway:latest` gives the image a name:
       bigkola1     = your DockerHub username (change to yours)
       gateway      = service name
       latest       = version tag (you could use v1.0.0 instead)
  - The final argument `./api-gateway` tells Docker where the Dockerfile is.

  # Push to DockerHub
  docker push bigkola1/easy-gateway:latest
  docker push bigkola1/easy-frontend:latest
  docker push bigkola1/easy-user-service:latest
  docker push bigkola1/easy-product-service:latest
  docker push bigkola1/easy-order-service:latest

EXPLANATION:
  - `docker push` uploads the built image to DockerHub (a free registry).
  - Kubernetes (in AWS) will later download these images from there.
  - You must run `docker login` once beforehand so DockerHub knows who you are.

