STEP 2 — CREATE EKS CLUSTER
-----------------------------

  eksctl create cluster \
    --name shopsphere-cluster \
    --region us-east-2 \
    --nodegroup-name standard-workers \
    --node-type t3.medium \
    --nodes 3 \
    --nodes-min 2 \
    --nodes-max 6 \
    --managed

EXPLANATION:
  - This single command creates a full Kubernetes cluster in AWS.
  - eksctl calls AWS CloudFormation behind the scenes to set up:
      - VPC (virtual network), subnets, security groups
      - An EKS control plane (the brain of Kubernetes, managed by AWS)
      - A node group (EC2 virtual machines that run your containers)
  - Flags explained:
    --name              = what you call your cluster
    --region            = which AWS data centre (us-east-2 = ohio)
    --nodegroup-name    = a label for your group of worker machines
    --node-type t3.medium = each worker is a t3.medium EC2 instance
                             (2 CPU, 4 GB RAM — good for small apps)
    --nodes 3           = start with 3 workers
    --nodes-min 2       = at least 2 workers (for redundancy)
    --nodes-max 6       = can auto-scale up to 6 if traffic increases
    --managed           = AWS manages the node group (easier updates)

  # Update kubeconfig (if needed)
  aws eks update-kubeconfig --region us-east-2 --name shopsphere-cluster

EXPLANATION:
  - This tells kubectl how to connect to your new cluster.
  - It writes the cluster's address and authentication token to your
    ~/.kube/config file. After this, `kubectl get nodes` works.

