STEP 3 — INSTALL AWS LOAD BALANCER CONTROLLER
-----------------------------------------------

  # Create IAM OIDC provider (if not already done)
  eksctl utils associate-iam-oidc-provider --region us-east-2 --cluster shopsphere-cluster

EXPLANATION:
  - This lets the cluster "prove its identity" to AWS.
  - OIDC (OpenID Connect) is a trust relationship: your cluster can
    request temporary AWS credentials for specific pods (e.g. the
    Load Balancer Controller) without storing secret keys.
  - Must be done once per cluster.

  # Download and apply the ALB controller policy
  curl -O https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes-sigs/aws-load-balancer-controller/v2.12.0/docs/install/iam_policy.json
  aws iam create-policy \
    --policy-name AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy \
    --policy-document file://iam_policy.json

EXPLANATION:
  - Downloads the exact permissions the Load Balancer Controller needs.
  - Creates an IAM Policy (a list of API actions it's allowed to do,
    like `elasticloadbalancing:CreateLoadBalancer`).
  - This policy will be attached to the controller's service account.

  # Create a service account for the controller
  eksctl create iamserviceaccount \
    --cluster shopsphere-cluster \
    --namespace kube-system \
    --name aws-load-balancer-controller \
    --role-name AmazonEKSLoadBalancerControllerRole \
    --attach-policy-arn arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:policy/AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy \
    --approve

EXPLANATION:
  - A Kubernetes Service Account + an IAM Role are created together.
  - The pod (Load Balancer Controller) will assume this role to call
    AWS APIs (like creating an ALB).
  - This is the secure way to give AWS permissions to a pod without
    hard-coding any keys.

  # Install the ALB controller via Helm
  helm repo add eks https://aws.github.io/eks-charts
  helm repo update
  helm install aws-load-balancer-controller eks/aws-load-balancer-controller \
    --namespace kube-system \
    --set clusterName=shopsphere-cluster \
    --set serviceAccount.create=false \
    --set serviceAccount.name=aws-load-balancer-controller

EXPLANATION:
  - Helm is a package manager for Kubernetes (like apt for Linux).
  - This command installs the ALB Controller as a pod in your cluster.
  - The controller watches for Kubernetes Ingress resources and
    automatically provisions real AWS Application Load Balancers.
  - serviceAccount.create=false + serviceAccount.name=... tells it to
    use the IAM service account we just created (not create a new one).

  # Verify it's running
  kubectl get deployment -n kube-system aws-load-balancer-controller

EXPLANATION:
  - Quick check that the controller pod is deployed and running.
  - You should see 1/1 READY.

